Upstream LSd modules, patched. Copied over upstream's at every start.

  sel.lua      /sel takes corners as numbers, and sel_begin() for
               the editor gamemode. Pending upstream. Its state is the
               module's own locals.
  lib_l10n.lua Every message in the player's language: per-script
               gettext catalogs (locales/<lang>.po), messages translated
               per recipient by server_msg and send_chat, /lang. Keeps
               upstream's API; what it replaces (the message tables, the
               lookup) is upstream's locals. Ours; not meant for upstream
               as is.
  command_cmds.lua
               /cmds, /help and /apropos show each command's
               description in the reader's language ("command"
               context). Each line is built by the local print_cmd,
               which does not know the reader. Re-apply onto upstream's
               after a bump.
  motd.lua     Each motd line in the player's language: a line may be
               a language map, and %(server) is the server's name. Not
               told to a bot when it joins (people_in). The lines are
               split inside upstream's local send_motd.
  boundary_damage.lua
               A map's boundary_damage written over several lines (as
               many on the web are) was read as empty, and its tick
               compared positions with nil every tick -- the error ended
               each tick, stopping every script after it (bots, blasts).
               Reads the block whole; an incomplete one is none. The
               parse is upstream's own after.pyscrape_ext. Report
               upstream (lib_pyscrape's get_ext reads one line only).
  afkick.lua   A bot is never away from its keyboard: it is given no
               timer, so its inputs -- hundreds a second in a fleet --
               cost nothing (upstream walks every player three times
               for each). The one rule for it: lib_bot fakes no life
               for afkick. Its timers are local and run inside its own
               input hooks. Ours (bots are lib_bot's).

  tentspawns.lua
               A spawn is on land near the tent's level: not on a thin
               layer floating far above the tent (a slab in the sky over
               air deeper than a safe fall: keidoro's at z 0), not in the
               sea at a cliff's foot (32 draws, then the tent's own column),
               and never on a column off the map (the square is cut to it).
               Upstream's draw and height are one function. Report upstream.

  map_meta.lua The name of the next map is told to the people (lib_bot's
               people_in), not to each bot: upstream's loop over every id
               is inside its map load hook.

  purgatory.lua, command_ignore.lua, command_secspec.lua, command_ups.lua
               Upstream split every packet a script sent to a group
               (a broadcast, a team) into one send for each id of it,
               bots too: 230 calls and 230 packets for each packet.
               A group goes whole, once, unless someone in it needs a
               different one (a player in purgatory, one who ignores the
               sender, the spectator who is spoken of, a /ups rate); then
               only the people of it (lib_bot's people_in) are walked.
               The split is each module's own loop, called below any hook
               above it. The rule is lib_bot's THE BROADCASTER;
               tests/bot_sends_test.lua. command_ups also sends 30 World
               Updates a second by default, not 60 (ups_default_rate).

Each one is a whole file, so it can drift from upstream silently. A
change that can be made from outside -- a hook on the chain, in the
module that owns the rule -- is made there instead. What is left here
could not be (a local function changed, a loop inside the module that
has to be a different loop): each entry above says which.

So each file's second line names the upstream file it was made from,
by a checksum (-- upstream: lsd/scripts/<file> fnv1a32 ...), and each
change in it is marked (lsd-docker, PATCHED). tests/overrides_test.lua
fails the moment upstream's file is not that one any more: after
`dev/bump-lsd.sh`, re-apply the patch onto upstream's new copy
(git -C lsd diff OLD..NEW -- scripts/<file>) and write the checksum the
test prints.
